WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.060
- I'm Ali, and this is my son, Rafael.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.070
I am hoping to learn
American Sign Language

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.470
over the next 60 days.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.963
Yay!

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.690
When Rafael was born, he
failed his newborn hearing

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.060
screening at the hospital.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.840
At six weeks old, we
were given the diagnosis

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.320
that he has profound
unilateral hearing loss

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.950
in his right ear, which
means he is completely deaf

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:24.930
in his right ear, and
that means there's a lot

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:25.763
of question marks.

00:00:25.763 --> 00:00:27.070
He does have normal
hearing in his left ear,

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.930
but I've talked to parents
all over the country

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.720
with children with
unilateral hearing loss,

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.480
and their experiences are vastly different

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.880
in terms of communication.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.860
Some kids do totally fine
with just one working ear.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.030
Some kids rely completely
on American Sign Language

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:42.462
as their form of
communication, and some lie

00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:43.610
somewhere in between.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.460
When we got that first
diagnosis from the audiologist,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.630
the experience there was a little bit dry.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.370
You know, she sort of
gave us the diagnosis

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.180
and then immediately tried to sell us

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.870
a certain brand of hearing aid.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.240
She sorta presented it as
this is the only solution.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.930
She sent us to an ENT and we didn't have

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.090
a great experience there either.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.270
We felt sort of very lost after that.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:07.080
We decided to get a second opinion,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.410
so we went to Children's Hospital,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.508
and we found an audiologist
who gave us the same diagnosis

00:01:11.508 --> 00:01:14.188
but talked to us more
about different options.

00:01:14.188 --> 00:01:15.488
We felt more listened to.

00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:17.800
And the very next day, I got a phone call

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:20.410
from someone reaching out
to me, saying, you know,

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:24.070
we received your son's
diagnosis and we're gonna

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:26.520
put you in touch with a
group called ParentLinks,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.540
which is a community
for parents of children

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.090
with hearing loss, so you can connect

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.940
with other parents who are
going through the same thing.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.810
It's been incredible
to be enveloped by this

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.130
amazing community of parents and educators

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.030
and people who are advocates
for children with hearing loss.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.820
That's one of the reasons
why I'm really interested

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.010
in learning ASL is just
to give him another tool,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:46.710
another way of communicating with him.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.725
I just wanna give him every
opportunity to succeed.

00:01:49.725 --> 00:01:51.623
I think he's extraordinary.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.200
At the very beginning, I started taking

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.420
some online ASL lessons, and
at first, it was very basic.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.750
It was just sort of like words.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.610
So after about a week
and a half of online,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.203
I'm learning a lot of vocabulary words.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.261
I was sick last week, so
I know that I was sick.

00:02:07.261 --> 00:02:08.870
The hard part for me right now,

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.280
and the frustrating is like
putting it all together

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.700
and trying to create sentences,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.810
because the grammar and
the sentence structure

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.050
is very, very different.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.700
Every time I look at my
kid and I see that he knows

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:18.652
the sign for more.

00:02:18.652 --> 00:02:20.319
More, you want more?

00:02:20.353 --> 00:02:24.730
Or I think about how, yes,
it might be difficult,

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.600
but I wanna keep going and I wanna be able

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.920
to help him communicate more.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.859
It was really hard learning online only,

00:02:29.859 --> 00:02:33.970
because I didn't have anyone
to sort of like feedback with.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.695
And then I actually started
working with an ASL tutor, Loni.

00:02:36.695 --> 00:02:40.270
We just had our first
meeting and it was really

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:41.500
intimidating at first.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.147
As soon as I saw her, I said,

00:02:43.147 --> 00:02:47.660
"Hi, I'm Ali, nice to meet you."

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.950
You know, and she was
happy that I knew that.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.700
But then, I just sort of forgot myself

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:52.600
and started talking to her.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.127
I said, "We're gonna go
to Kate's desk right now

00:02:54.127 --> 00:02:56.337
"and get some paperwork,"
and she had to remind me,

00:02:56.337 --> 00:02:58.980
"I'm deaf," but she made
me feel at ease right away,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.830
'cause she could tell
that I was very nervous.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.256
I did a lot of writing things down today,

00:03:02.256 --> 00:03:04.460
because I was a little
overwhelmed, overwhelmed.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.240
I know that's okay, sort
of, at the beginning,

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.960
but I wanna be respectful of Loni

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.840
and not sort of have to write
everything down all the time.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.690
There was really great,
because actually having

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.710
one-on-one time to go back and
forth and practice my signs

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.995
with an actual person
who knows the language,

00:03:18.995 --> 00:03:22.284
it was just like a whole
'nother world of learning.

00:03:22.284 --> 00:03:25.450
So I'm gonna quiz myself
on some of these flashcards

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.180
that Loni and I have been working on.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.820
I will not look, but I'll show you.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.849
So this sign is daughter.

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:32.688
(bell rings)

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.100
So I'm about halfway through.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.190
One of the things I really
need to start working on now,

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:39.703
too, is to actually have conversation.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.140
And that's where it gets
a little bit tricky,

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.440
because ASL is its own language.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:44.280
There's two different things.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.807
There's signed English, which is,

00:03:45.807 --> 00:03:50.590
"Do you want a banana or an apple?"

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.523
But in ASL, that same
sentence would simply just be,

00:03:54.523 --> 00:03:58.377
"Apple, banana, which?"

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.130
That's a really rudimentary example,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.680
but a lot of things like
that are very tricky

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.210
to sort of get the grammar and moving

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.610
things around into sentences.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.063
This is Loni.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.423
She's my tutor for ASL.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.034
It's important that I have a tutor

00:04:12.034 --> 00:04:15.020
because part of the grammar
is your facial expressions,

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.571
so it really helps to have another person.

00:04:17.571 --> 00:04:20.154
(bright music)

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.927
One of the first things
I learned how to say is,

00:04:35.927 --> 00:04:36.940
"How do you say?"

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.227
So I was trying my hardest
to be able to communicate

00:04:40.227 --> 00:04:42.470
only using sign.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.020
It's been a little over 60
days since this whole endeavor

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.280
started, and I'm feeling
really good about my progress.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.510
I feel like I'm finally starting

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.050
to like put sentences together.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.190
I'm getting really excited to start

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.374
actually speaking in ASL.

00:04:55.374 --> 00:04:57.957
(bright music)

00:05:19.265 --> 00:05:20.900
And now that I've had
sort of a taste for it,

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.400
I'm really falling in
love with the language.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.160
I feel like this is something
that's gonna bond us,

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.362
and it's going to make him understand

00:05:28.362 --> 00:05:30.820
that sort of no matter what he needs,

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:33.017
he'll always be able
to communicate with me.

00:05:33.017 --> 00:05:35.600
(bright music)

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.450
- Hey, Unsolved is on a new channel.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:18.283
And now your part.

00:06:18.283 --> 00:06:19.180
- [Both] Subscribe here.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:20.173
- That was my part.

